The problem is a classic "selecting items with decreasing value" problem. At each step, we want to pick the index that gives the maximum marginal gain.

The marginal gain for index `i` on its `t-th` selection (where `t` is 1-indexed) is `value[i] - decay[i] * (t - 1)`.

So at any point, the best index to select is the one with the highest current marginal gain.

However, my current greedy approach sorts indices by `value[i] - decay[i]` once at the beginning, and then iterates through that list each time. But the correct greedy approach should, at each step, pick the index with the highest current marginal gain among all indices.

